1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,740
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our orthodontic series.

2
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:13,000
 So in this video we are going to cover a lot of orthodontic appliances.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,560
 So I'm going to try to stick to the highest yield big picture facts associated with each

4
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,000
 of these appliances.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,560
 So first let's talk about types of orthodontic treatment.

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:32,240
 So while patient is young and growing, we can try to modify how their jaws are growing,

7
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:36,040
 addressing an underlying skeletal discrepancy.

8
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:42,100
 Now when the patient is older and has stopped growing, the only way to address a true skeletal

9
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:46,640
 issue at its source is with surgical correction.

10
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:51,920
 But the vast majority of treatment falls somewhere in the middle, either not taking teeth out

11
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:58,100
 with non-extraction or taking teeth out with extraction, either way moving teeth around

12
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,480
 within the jaws that you have.

13
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:04,440
 So these are purely dental, alveolar changes.

14
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:09,800
 And importantly, if there's an underlying skeletal discrepancy, either of these treatment

15
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:15,040
 options is simply camouflaging the skeletal problem with dental change.

16
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:20,920
 So I think this is a really helpful way, a big picture view of looking at the four main

17
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,060
 categories of orthodontic treatment.

18
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:29,880
 And we'll be focusing on the first three of these in this video.

19
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:35,600
 So successful growth modification can only occur during periods of active growth.

20
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:42,160
 And that's generally from 8 to 13 for girls and let's say 10 to 15 for boys.

21
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:45,480
 But of course, there's individual variation there.

22
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:50,800
 And this is really why we spent a whole video talking about growth and development because

23
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:58,920
 it does come into play in the clinic in terms of when and how we can do growth modification.

24
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:07,800
 So class 2 headgear restrains maxillary growth forward, functional appliances, stimulate

25
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:13,720
 mandibular growth forward, and both of these are targeted towards class 2 patients.

26
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:20,360
 And then we have reverse pull headgear, which stimulates maxillary growth forward.

27
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:25,280
 And truthfully, there's really no good way to restrain mandibular growth, although a

28
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:28,320
 chin cup has been used to attempt that.

29
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:34,120
 And both of these are targeted towards helping class 3 patients.

30
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:42,040
 So we're going to unpack each of these categories in the next several slides.

31
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:48,640
 So headgear is best used during the ascending portion of the pubertal growth spurt.

32
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,080
 Another way to say that is the patient is pre-pubertal.

33
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:58,480
 And headgear can be used to modify growth of the jaws or sometimes to reinforce anchorage.

34
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:03,320
 But again, we're going to be focusing on the growth modification piece.

35
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:10,400
 And most of these should be worn preferentially 12 to 14 hours per day in order to hit that

36
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:13,480
 threshold for an effect on the jaws.

37
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:18,480
 So compliance is a huge issue with headgear.

38
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:26,340
 High or occipital pull headgear involves a face bow that connects or hooks into headgear

39
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:29,680
 tubes on the upper molar bands.

40
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:36,680
 You have a head strap at the back of the head and then this elastic band that applies force.

41
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:44,520
 And we can actually literally see that force vector that's being applied to the face bow

42
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:48,200
 and indirectly to the teeth.

43
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,360
 So what is this doing?

44
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:55,920
 Well, it's restraining maxillary forward growth, but it's also having a dental effect.

45
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:03,320
 And it's intruding and distalizing those upper molars thanks to that upward force vector.

46
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:10,800
 And so it's best for class two open bite patients, class two because it's restraining maxillary

47
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:16,520
 growth and open bite because it's intruding posterior teeth.

48
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:24,040
 And if it's intruding posterior teeth, that helps to close a bite down in the front.

49
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:30,880
 For a low or cervical pull headgear, that involves, again, a face bow and elastic band.

50
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:37,760
 But this time, there's a neck strap, so the force vector is pointing down this time.

51
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:41,200
 So we're going to restrain maxillary forward growth.

52
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:45,480
 This time we're going to extrude and distalize those upper molars.

53
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:51,400
 So this one's going to be best for class two deep bite patients because extruding posterior

54
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:56,880
 teeth helps to open a bite in the front.

55
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:01,040
 J-hook headgear looks kind of similar to the headgear we've already looked at.

56
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:08,840
 But this time, a J-shaped hook engages into a loop in the arch wire, usually by the upper

57
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:10,680
 lateral or canine.

58
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:18,640
 So this provides extra oral anchorage to retract the canines and incisors and is most effective

59
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:22,680
 in maximum anchorage cases.

60
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:29,400
 So the target for this one is dento alveolar and it's actually not growth modification

61
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,560
 in this case.

62
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:38,000
 Reverse pull headgear is called such because it's pulling in the opposite direction.

63
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,480
 We're not pulling back, we're actually pulling forward.

64
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:46,880
 It's also sometimes called a face mask because it's located in front of the face.

65
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:52,520
 So this is going to stimulate maxillary forward growth with that forward pull.

66
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:59,200
 Other effects often undesirable include protraction of the upper incisors, retraction of the

67
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:05,040
 lower incisors and clockwise rotation of the mandible.

68
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:11,440
 Now it's best for class three patients with a deficient maxilla because it's really mostly

69
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,800
 targeting the maxilla.

70
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:19,640
 This treatment should be done really as soon as those permanent first molars and incisors

71
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:26,600
 erupt and we want to do it really early on because we want those circummaxillary sutures,

72
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:31,360
 the sutures around the maxilla to be as pliable and loose as possible.

73
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:38,680
 So really we could do this as soon as age six to eight or soon after that.

74
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:44,040
 So the chin cup involves extra oral force to the mandible anchored against the back of

75
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:50,480
 the head and this is to restrain mandibular growth at the condyles.

76
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:57,000
 So this has been shown to restrain mandibular growth in animal studies where monkeys would

77
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,720
 wear the appliance for 24 hours a day but in humans this simply doesn't work.

78
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:09,960
 And the result is instead of restraining mandibular growth, we're really getting clockwise and

79
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:14,720
 open rotation of the mandible rather than true growth restraints.

80
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:21,960
 So this one I say best and a loosely, it's best for class three mandibular excess patients

81
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:26,760
 with the caveat that it doesn't really work all that well.

82
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:31,600
 All right, the next category are the functional appliances.

83
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:37,040
 And functional appliances like headgear are best used during the ascending portion of

84
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:42,320
 the puberdal growth spurt and the idea is that they influence the normal function of

85
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:48,600
 the mandible and these appliances posture the mandible into a protrusive position and

86
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:55,240
 the resultant stretch of the facial muscles and soft tissue modify growth at the condyles

87
00:07:55,240 --> 00:08:00,280
 and you can feel that kind of stretch if you push your lower jaw forward.

88
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:07,080
 So these are all class two correctors, particularly patients with a mandibular deficiency because

89
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:11,840
 this time we're really focusing on that mandible.

90
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:15,000
 So most of these functional appliances are removable.

91
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,840
 So again, like headgear, patient compliance plays a major, major role.

92
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:28,760
 So the biinator involves a horseshoe shaped acrylic that's located between the upper and

93
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:34,840
 lower teeth and it guides the patient into a position where the mandible is advanced

94
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:40,960
 forward so the lower teeth can clear this block of plastic.

95
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:47,960
 In addition, this labial bow here anterior to the maxillary incisors tries to eliminate

96
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:53,240
 pressure from the upper lip and buckle musculature.

97
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:58,920
 So with the mandible being held forward, the thought is bone growth at the condyles is

98
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:04,840
 going to be accelerated to fill in the extra space at the jaw joint.

99
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:10,880
 The biinator has some pros, it's a simple design, it's fairly durable, has a lot of

100
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:18,720
 hardware to keep all these pieces intact and it's generally readily accepted by patients.

101
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:25,380
 The activator is similar is actually the first removable functional appliance developed and

102
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:31,880
 the idea is very similar to the biinator that the activation of the mandibular musculature

103
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:36,040
 is important in obtaining a growth effect.

104
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:42,640
 The lingual flanges of this appliance design extend deeper than it does in the biinator

105
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:49,440
 which encourages that forward posture to avoid this impinging on the floor of the mouth but

106
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:57,960
 this also means it's generally less well received and more uncomfortable than the biinator is.

107
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:04,320
 So a twin block can be fixed, it can be cemented in place or it can be removable like the previous

108
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:12,480
 two and if it's fixed on the teeth, it can be cemented in place at the molars or sometimes

109
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:13,640
 even the premolars.

110
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:20,360
 If it's removable, it's retained on the teeth with conventional clasps as seen here.

111
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:27,400
 So these plastic blocks on the upper and lower portions can be used to control eruption of

112
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:29,600
 the teeth which gives you an added benefit.

113
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:34,340
 You can try to block eruption of certain teeth if you're worried about vertical issues but

114
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:39,600
 they also work as part of the functional appliance because they have these inclines.

115
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:45,000
 So these inclines on the upper and lower blocks force the patient to advance the mandible

116
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,120
 in order to close all the way.

117
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:52,280
 You can imagine if this dentiform has an advanced all the way and this lower block is located

118
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:56,760
 here, they're going to bang up against each other and the patient can't close together

119
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:00,760
 which is a really uncomfortable feeling.

120
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:03,800
 So that is essentially how the twin block works.

121
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:12,260
 It forces you to protrude this mandible forward and you have some tension created soft tissue

122
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:18,900
 pull and the oral musculature and then you have space back at the condyle and the thought

123
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:23,240
 is it's accelerating bone growth at that condyle.

124
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:28,520
 The pros of the twin block is that it provides more positive mandibular changes than the previous

125
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:37,080
 two this is according to many class two studies that have been conducted in the literature.

126
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:44,760
 The MARA is a mandibular anterior repositioning appliance, that's what it stands for and this

127
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:50,480
 is a fixed appliance, it was created in the 1990s so this is actually a pretty recent and

128
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:58,080
 it requires the patient to again advance their mandible in order to close otherwise the upper

129
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:04,060
 elbow that's this part that's connected to the upper molar tube interferes with the lower

130
00:12:04,060 --> 00:12:05,600
 fixed arm.

131
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:09,880
 So it's again very similar to the ones we've already talked about, you have to actively

132
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:15,240
 advance the mandible in order to clear the interference and close all the way.

133
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:20,280
 The pros with this one is it's less bulky, it's more durable and more stable than the

134
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:22,800
 hertz appliance which is on the next slide.

135
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:27,760
 The cons are that you get generally less mandibular advancement than the twin block which is what

136
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:32,600
 we just talked about and the hertz which we'll talk about right now.

137
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:38,720
 The hertz is another fixed appliance, this one was created in the early 1900s but it

138
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:41,680
 was popularized later in the century.

139
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:48,600
 So this time we have a piston and a tube device that can be adjusted when the patient comes

140
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:56,400
 in for appointments and it literally pushes the mandible forward as the patient closes.

141
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:59,800
 So this one is passively pushing the mandible forward.

142
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:05,400
 The patient doesn't have to actively activate their muscles to close in that way.

143
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:09,240
 The appliance is essentially doing the work for them.

144
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:13,520
 So besides not breaking it, there's actually minimal compliance here because again the

145
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:17,560
 appliance does all the work of advancing the mandible.

146
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:24,840
 So forcing the patient to maintain an advanced mandibular position can generate both skeletal

147
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,920
 and dental changes.

148
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:32,680
 But keep in mind that all of these appliances while we want skeletal change, they're going

149
00:13:32,680 --> 00:13:35,480
 to produce some amount of dental change as well.

150
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:43,960
 Typically, you're going to get some upper distal and intrusive force and lower intrusive

151
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:45,760
 and mesial force.

152
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:53,800
 You're also going to get some proclination of these lower incisors and retroclination

153
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:55,400
 of these upper incisors.

154
00:13:55,400 --> 00:14:01,440
 That's kind of the classic class two mechanics in these functional appliances.

155
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:07,960
 For the board exam, I wouldn't worry about all of those small minute details and focus

156
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:13,720
 more on the big picture stuff, kind of like we've been talking about so far.

157
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:19,360
 So I'm calling these two other class two correctors because, well, they're not really functional

158
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:21,320
 appliances for growth modification.

159
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,560
 Their effects are almost entirely dental alveolar.

160
00:14:25,560 --> 00:14:32,980
 I'd probably also put class two elastics or rubber bands in this category as well.

161
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:39,640
 So the force's appliance is another fixed appliance and it's a push rod spring.

162
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:47,640
 So this push rod can be assembled intraorally by attaching it to the headgear tube on the

163
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:54,920
 upper molar and then to the lower arch wire by the premolar canine area.

164
00:14:54,920 --> 00:15:00,320
 And it's not going to get the same skeletal effect because it's not holding the mandible

165
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:01,320
 forward.

166
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:05,720
 It's not forcing you to push the mandible forward, but you do get similar tooth movement

167
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:13,720
 effects in that there are upper intrusive and distalization forces and lower intrusion

168
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:20,440
 and musial forces applied by the spring that's pushing in both directions.

169
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:25,880
 The pros are it's non-compliant and doesn't require patient compliance other than not

170
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:32,240
 breaking it, of course, and it's often the go-to if patients aren't wearing their elastics

171
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:34,800
 properly.

172
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:40,120
 More maxillary restriction, that's probably in relation to the Herpes appliance, which

173
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:46,160
 you're theoretically getting more mandibular advancement than you are maxillary restriction.

174
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:53,520
 As far as cons, it requires heavy upper and lower arch wires in order to avoid some more

175
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:57,320
 undesirable side effects in terms of dental movements.

176
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:02,320
 So you really need to have some pretty good arch wires to avoid some unwanted rotations

177
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:05,200
 and things like that.

178
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:10,440
 The pendulum appliance is a fixed appliance, although I have seen some versions that are

179
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:19,480
 removable, and this one is banded to the upper first molars for the distalization and de-rotation

180
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:21,160
 of those molars.

181
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:27,480
 So you have this acrylic button on the pallet with wire rests that are bonded to the premolars

182
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:35,000
 for anchorage, and then you have these helical springs that provide a light, continuous pressure

183
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,520
 to push those molars back.

184
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:43,920
 So that is a localized form of Class II correction at the molars.

185
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:51,960
 You can also ask for a Pindex appliance that's pendulum and expander combined if you want

186
00:16:51,960 --> 00:16:58,040
 to use a jack screw expander for broadening that arch at the same time.

187
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:01,360
 We'll cover expanders in just a little bit here.

188
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:04,560
 In fact, let's cover in the next slide.

189
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:10,600
 So you may be sensing a theme here, but palatable expanders are best used during the ascending

190
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:13,560
 portion of the pubertal growth spurt.

191
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:23,280
 Once again, while those sutures are pliable and growth is actively occurring.

192
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:31,240
 So the Shwarz appliance is a removable appliance, and it has a jack screw located right here

193
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:36,720
 at the midline between the left and the right plates.

194
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:42,320
 Now with this kind of appliance, the patient's parent or guardian is responsible for taking

195
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:48,280
 a small key that comes with the appliance and turning this jack screw.

196
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:58,560
 And one turn is going to equal 0.25 or 1/4 of a millimeter of expansion.

197
00:17:58,560 --> 00:18:06,120
 So one turn is 0.25 millimeters, and so you may do one turn a day, you may do one turn

198
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:12,400
 every other day, you may do two turns a day, depends how aggressive you want to expand.

199
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:19,240
 Now the problem with this one is it's removable, so failure to wear the appliance, even for

200
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:24,960
 just a day, might require adjustment of the jack screw in order to constrict it back the

201
00:18:24,960 --> 00:18:30,720
 other way until it fits the mouth, because this transverse expansion can relapse very

202
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:36,920
 quickly, so even one day of not wearing it, it might not fit the next day.

203
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:42,040
 With this appliance, you're really only getting dental tipping, that's tipping of these teeth

204
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:48,760
 out, and you're not really getting true skeletal expansion at the mid-palatal suture, especially

205
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:55,560
 if you're using this in the lower arch, because mandibular skeletal expansion is impossible,

206
00:18:55,560 --> 00:19:01,600
 because there's no suture in the mandible, so a lower source especially will only accomplish

207
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:03,880
 dental tipping.

208
00:19:03,880 --> 00:19:11,880
 This appliance is only realistically good for treating mild posterior cross-pights.

209
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:18,360
 The W arch, because it looks like a W, is a fixed appliance, and this one's banded to

210
00:19:18,360 --> 00:19:25,080
 the molars, so you get this compressed spring that's activated when you push it into the

211
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:26,080
 mouth.

212
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:31,240
 So really, it's looking something more like this, and when we go to squeeze it in there,

213
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:36,320
 we have to squeeze those arms so they're activated and they're delivering pressure to the right

214
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:37,880
 and to the left.

215
00:19:37,880 --> 00:19:42,680
 It's delivering a few hundred grams of force and provides slow expansion, so generally

216
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:50,320
 pretty light force in terms of skeletal expansion, so you're realistically getting about one-thirds

217
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:56,400
 skeletal expansion and two-thirds dental expansion, but once again you're getting mostly dental

218
00:19:56,400 --> 00:20:01,320
 tipping but a little bit more skeletal expansion than the schwarz plate.

219
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:07,920
 It's more effective and more comfortable and generally more efficient than that split plate

220
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:11,240
 or schwarz appliance.

221
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:17,280
 The quad helix is another fixed appliance, and it's named because there are four helical

222
00:20:17,280 --> 00:20:18,280
 loops.

223
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:26,240
 We have one here, two, three, and four, so two in the anterior and two in the posterior.

224
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:33,180
 It's similar to the W arch, is it still kind of looks like a W, but it's capable of applying

225
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:40,720
 force more to the anterior or more to the posterior depending on how it's activated thanks

226
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:42,720
 to these four loops.

227
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:48,880
 So if you activate the front ones more, you're going to get more posterior expansion.

228
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:54,120
 If you activate the posterior ones more, you're going to get more anterior expansion, so it

229
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:58,360
 just depends on how you choose to activate those loops.

230
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:03,800
 If it's in for a long time, it's kind of interesting, both the W arch and the quad helix can actually

231
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:10,560
 leave an imprint on the dorsal surface of the tongue.

232
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:15,320
 So the high racks is one of the more common expanders used today.

233
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:22,260
 It's another fixed appliance, and the bands are usually cemented on the molars, the first

234
00:21:22,260 --> 00:21:25,480
 molars, as well as the first premolars.

235
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:32,680
 Again, this is involving a jack screw expander right here at the center, and one turn each

236
00:21:32,680 --> 00:21:38,880
 day is enough to produce up to a hundred newtons of force and expand the pallet effectively

237
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:41,760
 by opening that mid-palatal suture.

238
00:21:41,760 --> 00:21:47,000
 You can really tell that that suture is opening up when you see a diastema between eight and

239
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
 nine opening up as well.

240
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:55,600
 So the pros of this one are you're going to get, you will get effective skeletal expansion,

241
00:21:55,600 --> 00:21:59,360
 as well as that patient is at the right age.

242
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:04,560
 Cons are, it's bulky, it's more difficult to place and remove because of it.

243
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:10,760
 The patient also may have trouble cleaning, so it can lead to some real bad soft tissue

244
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:17,160
 irritation of the pallet, and the patient or parent must activate the appliance.

245
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:23,520
 So again, we're depending on compliance, whereas with the quad helix and W arch, this

246
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:26,840
 bent wire does all the work for us.

247
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:32,920
 The high racks, any kind of jack screw appliance, we have to depend on the parent and guardian

248
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:36,240
 doing the key turning.

249
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:39,960
 Ahaz is the same as the high racks.

250
00:22:39,960 --> 00:22:47,160
 It's a fixed appliance with a jack screw expander, except it has two big acrylic pads

251
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:55,040
 that contact the palatal mucosa, which some clinicians feel maximizes that skeletal expansion.

252
00:22:55,040 --> 00:23:00,680
 Because these pads are contacting the palatal tissue, so you're getting this tissue-borne

253
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:05,800
 effect, not just a tooth-borne appliance like with the high racks.

254
00:23:05,800 --> 00:23:11,600
 The problem is, this one can be really hard to keep clean, and so you can get some really

255
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:14,560
 nasty tissue when you take this thing out.

256
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:23,000
 A whole bunch of food and stuff caught above this appliance is not a fun time.

257
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:30,040
 So with TPA or a trans palatal arch, or a lower lingual arch when it's used in the lower arches,

258
00:23:30,040 --> 00:23:35,200
 it's usually used to hold this transverse dimension steady.

259
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:39,800
 So this one's never really used as a palatal expander, I just wanted to include it here

260
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:46,600
 because it can be used that way, but it's usually used for preserving transverse anchorage,

261
00:23:46,600 --> 00:23:51,520
 which is a fancy way of saying holding that transverse dimension steady.

262
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:59,760
 It can be activated so it can be squeezed together and bonded in there at that activated position.

263
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:04,120
 It can also be used for some limited dental constriction as well if you do it the opposite

264
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:08,400
 way, although this is going to be just some very limited dental tipping.

265
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:13,280
 So again, this is usually used to preserve your transverse dimension.

266
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:18,120
 All right, now for some mixed dentition appliances.

267
00:24:18,120 --> 00:24:23,000
 Some things that we use primarily in the mixed dentition phase.

268
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:29,160
 All right, so the NANCE appliance is a very controversial appliance.

269
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:34,320
 Some people swear by it and others swear that it doesn't do a thing.

270
00:24:34,320 --> 00:24:39,880
 And the idea here is that you have an acrylic button that sits against the palette with wires

271
00:24:39,880 --> 00:24:43,320
 that run back to bands around the molars.

272
00:24:43,320 --> 00:24:50,280
 And this is supposed to hold the AP position of those upper molars and prevent them from

273
00:24:50,280 --> 00:24:52,080
 slipping forward.

274
00:24:52,080 --> 00:24:57,920
 So this could be used as a space maintainer if you have premature loss of a bunch of

275
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:04,120
 upper teeth, or it can be used during orthodontic treatment when closing space.

276
00:25:04,120 --> 00:25:10,800
 If you want to hold those molars back and again prevent them from slipping forward.

277
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:17,040
 So this is something you could use to reinforce anchorage in a maximum anchorage case.

278
00:25:17,040 --> 00:25:22,960
 Although again, some people, not a huge fan of this appliance.

279
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:26,560
 The lower lingual holding arch is something we've talked about before.

280
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:30,440
 It's similar to the NANCE, but it's used in the lower arch.

281
00:25:30,440 --> 00:25:34,960
 However, there's no place to put an acrylic button down here at the floor of the mouth.

282
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:40,880
 So instead, a wire is placed against the lingual surfaces of the incisors.

283
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:46,360
 Now this is actually a lot more effective because we have a hard stopping point instead

284
00:25:46,360 --> 00:25:50,480
 of this bouncy, palatal soft tissue.

285
00:25:50,480 --> 00:25:54,800
 So we would prefer, honestly, to use something like this in the upper arch.

286
00:25:54,800 --> 00:26:01,240
 The problem is, if we did, and we had a wire running along here, well, the lower teeth

287
00:26:01,240 --> 00:26:06,440
 would likely bite into it, and that would be a problem in and of itself.

288
00:26:06,440 --> 00:26:10,560
 So you're usually stuck with the NANCE in the upper arch, and we like to use a lower

289
00:26:10,560 --> 00:26:12,560
 lingual holding arch in the lower arch.

290
00:26:12,560 --> 00:26:20,520
 Anyway, the LLHA is used to preserve arch length by preventing mesial drift of those lower

291
00:26:20,520 --> 00:26:27,440
 molars and lingual tipping of those lower incisors during the transition from the mixed

292
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:34,560
 to the permanent dentition, and its main purpose is to hold open that leeway space.

293
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:43,320
 Again, it could be used in certain situations for some kind of maximum anchorage scenario.

294
00:26:43,320 --> 00:26:48,240
 It's designed to sit passively, but it can be activated if you want to tip those lower

295
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:51,560
 incisors forward, for example.

296
00:26:51,560 --> 00:26:57,640
 The lip bumper is a cool little appliance where this heavy arch wire, or I should say

297
00:26:57,640 --> 00:27:04,560
 heavy labial wire, is inserted into buckle tubes on the mandibular first molars, and

298
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:09,000
 this acrylic pad rests against the lower lip.

299
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:16,800
 So the lower lip transmits this force against the acrylic pad, and it tips these back molars

300
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:19,880
 distally, or backwards.

301
00:27:19,880 --> 00:27:25,000
 But not only that, it also allows those lower front teeth to lean forward, because we no

302
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:31,040
 longer have lip pressure from the mentalis pushing against those incisors.

303
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:37,200
 So a good way to summarize how this works is the lip bumper transmits lower lip pressure

304
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:43,360
 to the molars, tipping them back, and it relieves lower lip pressure from the incisors, allowing

305
00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:44,920
 them to tip forward.

306
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:49,540
 So it's creating space in both directions.

307
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:53,760
 As far as permanent dentition appliances, there's a lot more out there, but these are

308
00:27:53,760 --> 00:27:58,680
 two big categories, aligners and braces.

309
00:27:58,680 --> 00:28:04,360
 So aligners are clear and removable, and they involve a series of trays that are manufactured

310
00:28:04,360 --> 00:28:09,880
 according to a prescription that's developed by the provider using some kind of algorithm

311
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:15,720
 or software, and they're worn by the patient in a certain sequence.

312
00:28:15,720 --> 00:28:21,280
 And so bonded attachments are often required to aid in specific tooth movements.

313
00:28:21,280 --> 00:28:28,360
 And so if you have this clear plastic tray, sometimes a tooth is small or it's smooth,

314
00:28:28,360 --> 00:28:32,800
 and there's not a whole lot for the plastic to grab onto, and that tooth won't move in

315
00:28:32,800 --> 00:28:34,080
 the way we want.

316
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:40,480
 So sometimes we have to bond attachments, little blocks of composite onto a tooth to create

317
00:28:40,480 --> 00:28:46,880
 a tooth handle that the plastic can grab onto and help to move that tooth.

318
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:51,760
 Then of course we have braces, which is what everyone thinks of when they think of orthodontics,

319
00:28:51,760 --> 00:28:55,080
 can also be called fixed appliances.

320
00:28:55,080 --> 00:29:00,800
 The earliest we can use braces is usually with the eruption of the permanent first molars,

321
00:29:00,800 --> 00:29:06,160
 and the ideal for a comprehensive case is in the late mixed dentition or even better

322
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:09,400
 when all the permanent teeth have erupted.

323
00:29:09,400 --> 00:29:15,160
 In contemporary orthodontics, all of the teeth, including the molars, can be bonded with these

324
00:29:15,160 --> 00:29:16,160
 cool little brackets.

325
00:29:16,160 --> 00:29:22,440
 They used to put big metal bands around every single tooth, and now we have these neat little

326
00:29:22,440 --> 00:29:25,920
 brackets, and there's a whole bunch of different prescriptions of them.

327
00:29:25,920 --> 00:29:31,880
 There's MBT, Roth, Andrews, and all of that is way outside the scope of the board exam,

328
00:29:31,880 --> 00:29:37,200
 but just to give you an idea of a few of the different variations that are out there.

329
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:43,040
 So for applying brackets to the teeth, there's a set protocol that we usually follow.

330
00:29:43,040 --> 00:29:48,240
 The first thing is to clean off the enamel using pumice or a cotton roll to remove the

331
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:52,440
 pelicle in any plaque that might be on that tooth.

332
00:29:52,440 --> 00:29:59,360
 We etch the center of the clinical crown on that facial surface to allow for micro-mechanical

333
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:00,480
 bonding.

334
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:04,800
 This may sound familiar from our operative topics.

335
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:10,160
 Then we apply prime, which conditions the enamel, and will chemically bond to the composite

336
00:30:10,160 --> 00:30:14,560
 resin, which is placed onto the base of the bracket.

337
00:30:14,560 --> 00:30:18,800
 That's actually what's fixing it to the tooth surface.

338
00:30:18,800 --> 00:30:24,720
 So once that composite is placed on the bracket, the bracket is pushed onto the tooth and it's

339
00:30:24,720 --> 00:30:30,440
 centered at the center of the facial surface of the clinical crown, and then it's cured

340
00:30:30,440 --> 00:30:38,280
 with that blue light in order to polymerize the composite and fix the bracket in place.

341
00:30:38,280 --> 00:30:45,280
 So a big decision in orthodontic treatment planning is do we need to take any teeth out?

342
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:52,320
 That often comes down to, is there enough room in the arch for all the teeth to fit?

343
00:30:52,320 --> 00:30:56,920
 But honestly, there's a whole lot more that goes into that decision.

344
00:30:56,920 --> 00:31:00,000
 So we lean towards non-extraction.

345
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:06,160
 If the patient has minimal crowning or maybe even spacing available, if they have a deep

346
00:31:06,160 --> 00:31:13,920
 bite tendency, because non-extraction actually tends to open a bite up.

347
00:31:13,920 --> 00:31:19,240
 If they have flat retrusive lips, it also might make sense to do a non-extraction treatment

348
00:31:19,240 --> 00:31:25,720
 plan, because another way to think of non-extraction is expansion, or expanding the arch outward

349
00:31:25,720 --> 00:31:31,020
 and forward, and that can help to improve the soft tissue profile if they have flat

350
00:31:31,020 --> 00:31:36,520
 retrusive lips, same with obtuse nasolabial angle.

351
00:31:36,520 --> 00:31:42,240
 On the other side, we tend towards extraction if the patient has severe crowning.

352
00:31:42,240 --> 00:31:46,600
 They may not have enough room in the arch to fit all the teeth.

353
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:53,080
 If they have minimal overbite or even open bite, so the opposite is true here, if we

354
00:31:53,080 --> 00:31:57,560
 extract teeth that tends to deepen the bite.

355
00:31:57,560 --> 00:32:03,360
 If they have full, pertrusive lips, perhaps we don't want to expand the arches even more,

356
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:06,360
 same with acute nasolabial angle.

357
00:32:06,360 --> 00:32:13,720
 If they have anterior recession or minimal or thin attached gingival tissue, we probably

358
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:20,400
 don't want to stress the peridontal apparatus even more by pushing all those incisors even

359
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:21,960
 more forward.

360
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:28,080
 And if we're camouflaging a skeletal class 2 or class 3, we might take out teeth only

361
00:32:28,080 --> 00:32:34,400
 in one arch to help hide that underlying skeletal discrepancy.

362
00:32:34,400 --> 00:32:39,880
 In terms of comprehensive braces treatment, we typically think of our treatment plans

363
00:32:39,880 --> 00:32:42,040
 in 3 stages.

364
00:32:42,040 --> 00:32:43,960
 First is alignment and leveling.

365
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:49,400
 That's getting the teeth straight, leveling the curve of speed, improving overbite.

366
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:55,840
 Next we work on the molar relationship for AP correction and also close any space that

367
00:32:55,840 --> 00:33:00,120
 might be from extractions or already existent space.

368
00:33:00,120 --> 00:33:04,640
 And so a lot of this is done with chains and rubber bands, maybe if we're doing growth

369
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:08,600
 modification, we might use a hertz appliance, things like that.

370
00:33:08,600 --> 00:33:14,560
 And finally finishing and detailing, this involves a lot of root tip correction, so roots

371
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:19,360
 need to be paralleled to improve stability and peridontal health.

372
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:23,800
 And certain anterior teeth might look a little bit off in the smile, and so this is where

373
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:27,800
 we get really picky and finish the case.

374
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:35,640
 And finally for adult treatment, adults are more likely to request ceramic, lingual or

375
00:33:35,640 --> 00:33:38,880
 invisible braces, that just means aligners.

376
00:33:38,880 --> 00:33:45,600
 For a more aesthetic treatment, any peridontal conditions must be stabilized before beginning

377
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:47,120
 orthodontic treatment.

378
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:51,400
 That's really important because orthodontic tooth movement, like we've learned, is a

379
00:33:51,400 --> 00:33:57,800
 controlled inflammatory response, and if we have something like peridontal disease that's

380
00:33:57,800 --> 00:34:03,680
 uncontrolled inflammation, well that could really mess up what we're trying to do.

381
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:09,440
 Steel ligatures retain less plaque than elastomeric ligatures, that might be a good consideration

382
00:34:09,440 --> 00:34:13,360
 for someone with not the best oral hygiene.

383
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:18,320
 And finally, we can't do growth modification because there's no active growth and the growth

384
00:34:18,320 --> 00:34:19,920
 spurt is over.

385
00:34:19,920 --> 00:34:28,200
 So all inter-arch correction must be accomplished, mentally or surgically, since adults do not

386
00:34:28,200 --> 00:34:34,080
 have the benefit of modifying their growth during treatment.

387
00:34:34,080 --> 00:34:37,920
 So that's it for this video guys, thank you so much for watching and thanks for sticking

388
00:34:37,920 --> 00:34:44,400
 with it because I know this will be a long video because we covered a lot of different

389
00:34:44,400 --> 00:34:46,200
 treatment appliances.

390
00:34:46,200 --> 00:34:52,840
 I tried not to get too into the weeds with this and tried to keep with a more of an overview

391
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:57,280
 of the most important high yield facts for each of those appliances.

392
00:34:57,280 --> 00:35:01,760
 So please like this video if you enjoyed it and subscribe to this channel for much more

393
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:03,160
 on dentistry.

394
00:35:03,160 --> 00:35:08,320
 If you're interested in supporting this channel and what I do, please check out my Patreon page.

395
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:11,720
 Thank you to all of my patrons here for their support.

396
00:35:11,720 --> 00:35:16,880
 You can unlock things like access to my video slides to take notes on and practice questions

397
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:18,320
 for the board exams.

398
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:21,160
 So go check that out, the link is in the description.

399
00:35:21,160 --> 00:35:24,560
 Thanks again for watching everyone and I'll see you in the next video.

